WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.450
After years of rumors, we'll soon be getting
a follow up to one of the greatest shows in

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.680
TV history.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.159
El Camino A Breaking Bad Movie will debut
on Netflix on October 11th and we have a few

00:00:10.159 --> 00:00:12.400
ideas of what we'd like to see when it does.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.400
Breaking Bad callbacks

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.241
First things first: if El Camino is going
to truly satisfy fans of Breaking Bad, it

00:00:17.241 --> 00:00:19.400
has to feel like an extension of the series.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.589
The easiest way to do this would be through
the use of callbacks.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:26.710
Breaking Bad laid on the callbacks to previous
episodes early and often, a practice which

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.560
continued in the spin-off series Better Call
Saul.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.579
The callbacks in Breaking Bad were wildly
varied, from dialogue to clothing choices

00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:36.719
to music, and were used for a lot more than
just fan service.

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:40.270
"I knew this was going to happen.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.270
I knew it.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.660
I told you someday someone was going to come
to our door."

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.040
"I am the one who knocks!"

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.570
El Camino will only have a couple of hours
to tell its story, and we don't expect it

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.870
to spend its entire run time reminding us
that, holy cow, we're watching more Breaking

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:55.870
Bad.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.879
But deploying clever, subtle callbacks to
the series is one way in which El Camino can

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:00.129
honor the show's legacy.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:01.129
Saul Goodman

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:05.160
We get the feeling that El Camino won't feature
too many returning characters from Breaking

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.800
Bad, for the simple fact that the majority
of the more significant supporting players

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.880
are, well, dead.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.910
But thanks to Better Call Saul, we know that
"criminal lawyer" Saul Goodman got safely

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:18.050
out of town in the aftermath of White's downfall,
and ended up exactly where he predicted he

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.300
would: managing a Cinnabon in some nondescript
mall in Omaha.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.750
Each season of Better Call Saul opens with
a flash forward to Goodman's Cinnabon days,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.350
and while it's safe to say he won't be getting
sucked into the criminal underworld of Nebraska

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:33.780
anytime soon, that doesn't mean that El Camino
couldn't see Jesse Pinkman pay him a little

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.780
visit.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.270
After all, Goodman could always be depended
upon to bail Pinkman and White out of seemingly

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.640
impossible situations, and just because he's
laying low slinging cinnamon rolls doesn't

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.310
mean he's severed all of his ties to his former
criminal associates.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:46.560
Badger and Skinny Pete

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.210
If someone's going to fill in Jesse Pinkman
on what's been going down in Albuquerque since

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.990
he split, we'd bet it'll be his old buddies,
Badger and Skinny Pete.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.770
We already know that at the very least, Skinny
Pete will be involved in El Camino; the first

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.810
trailer to be released for the flick is simply
a scene of the former drug runner being questioned

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:02.810
by authorities.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.660
"No way I'm helping you people put Jesse Pinkman
back inside a cage."

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:10.909
It's not yet clear if Badger will also pop
up, but where one of these two goes, the other

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:15.569
usually follows - and the pair could serve
a purpose beyond merely doling out exposition.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:19.390
They're Pinkman's connection to Albuquerque,
where his parents still live - as does Brock,

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:23.250
the young son of Pinkman's murdered girlfriend
Andrea, and perhaps the one living person

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.760
he cares about the most.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.810
Badger and Skinny Pete might also warn Pinkman
of impending danger; White may have wiped

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.940
out the entirety of the Nazi gang that was
holding his former partner captive in Breaking

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.049
Bad's final episode, but that doesn't mean
said gang's associates won't be seeking recourse.

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:38.409
The new criminal element

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:42.459
White's utter destruction of that Nazi gang
by way of automated machine gun is not the

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:44.989
kind of action that just goes unnoticed.

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:48.950
Whatever slimy characters that gang was affiliated
with are sure to have taken notice of two

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.980
things: first, there's a void in the meth
production game that will need to be filled.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.000
Second, the two best meth cooks in New Mexico
were present at the scene of that carnage

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.819
- and one of them is dead, but the other is
most certainly not.

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:04.360
Which is to say, it may not just be the long
arm of the law that will take an interest

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.840
in Pinkman in El Camino.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.069
Breaking Bad gave us a long, harrowing look
at how drug empires operate, and Pinkman has

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:11.069
seen it all.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:15.510
He's been at the center of drug operations
from Tuco's mini empire to Madrigal's international

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.819
drugs clearing house and as the New Mexico
narcotics trade rebuilds itself in the wake

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:23.969
of Heisenberg and the Nazis' demise, there
are sure to be several parties interested

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:24.969
in his whereabouts.

00:03:24.969 --> 00:03:26.079
Peace for Jesse

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:29.310
Whatever road lies ahead for Pinkman, it won't
be an easy one.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:33.060
But we have to hope that it brings some kind
of closure, some kind of peace, because if

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.209
anyone on Earth ever needed it, it's him.

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:39.079
Sure, he's done terrible things, but perhaps
none of those things would have come to pass

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:43.219
if he had never been singled out as the ideal
partner by his former teacher Mr. White.

00:03:43.219 --> 00:03:47.239
And despite all of the admittedly terrible
choices he's made, Aaron Paul's incredible,

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:50.849
soulful performance had a way of painting
Pinkman as someone who always wanted to do

00:03:50.849 --> 00:03:53.650
the right thing, but for whatever reason,
always failed.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.650
"I'm not coming back."

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.359
"I know."

00:03:57.359 --> 00:04:01.349
Maybe he'll be given a chance to rectify the
transgressions of his past in El Camino.

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:05.090
This should at least be easier now that White,
who always seemed to be pushing Pinkman to

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.769
be the worst possible version of himself,
is out of the picture.

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:12.079
Then again, if you're going to cook up a Breaking
Bad movie, it'd be missing a key ingredient

00:04:12.079 --> 00:04:14.340
if it didn't acknowledge Jesse's departed
partner.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.340
Walter White

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.810
He is the danger; he is the one who knocks.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.750
Walter White is the black, infected heart
beating at the center of Breaking Bad, and

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.850
however it has to happen, it sure seems like
failing to include him in some capacity would

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:27.880
be doing El Camino a disservice.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.890
It could be a flashback, or a drug induced
vision; it could be a recording that White

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.840
made for Pinkman in the event of his death,
or even a home movie that we see Skyler White

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.450
watching, right before she throws it in the
fireplace.

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:42.280
There are a hundred different ways it could
happen, but the subtitle of El Camino is "A

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.890
Breaking Bad Movie" and if you're going to
offer us a continuation of one of the greatest

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:50.290
stories ever told on TV, you would be remiss
not to offer us at least a glimpse of that

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:51.810
story's greatest character.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.120
"Say my name."

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:54.920
"Heisenberg."

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.300
"You're god darn right."

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.980
Yes, El Camino is Pinkman's story, but the
character's greatest tragedy is that his story

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.720
will forever, no matter how much he would
will it to be otherwise, be intertwined with

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:08.720
White's.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.320
Make no mistake: there are plenty of fans
who would advance the theory that White didn't

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.770
really die at the conclusion of Breaking Bad,
but we're not among them.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:20.600
He is indeed dead, but the dark legacy that
he created will linger; as far as Pinkman

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.780
is concerned, we can only hope that he's able
to run far enough, fast enough, to somehow

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.840
escape it.

